1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,351
A small team should be able to shape its own tools. RAI OS explores that idea: describe

2
00:00:05,351 --> 00:00:10,452
a tool, review its behavior, and use it inside a Rust operating system.

3
00:00:12,250 --> 00:00:16,457
We start a new session and describe a tool for moving quantities between locations, with

4
00:00:16,457 --> 00:00:20,665
a total and a chart. These names and rules come from the application request. They are

5
00:00:20,665 --> 00:00:24,872
not special application types built into the kernel.

6
00:00:30,250 --> 00:00:34,290
This is an actual Meta request. The model receives the implemented capability catalog

7
00:00:34,290 --> 00:00:38,330
and proposes a declarative application. Rust validates its fields and operations. We

8
00:00:38,330 --> 00:00:42,370
review the transfer rule and the page before installation. A proposal alone cannot

9
00:00:42,370 --> 00:00:46,409
execute an operation.

10
00:00:52,250 --> 00:00:57,126
The generated form accepts our test records: twelve units in Warehouse, and five in

11
00:00:57,126 --> 00:01:02,003
Dispatch. We enter a quantity of four and choose Dispatch as the destination. These are

12
00:01:02,003 --> 00:01:06,879
fictional demonstration records, entered through the same guest form controls.

13
00:01:10,250 --> 00:01:14,131
The preview shows both changes before we apply them. Warehouse falls from twelve to

14
00:01:14,131 --> 00:01:18,011
eight, while Dispatch rises from five to nine. The total remains seventeen. Rust

15
00:01:18,011 --> 00:01:21,892
validates and commits both records together, and the chart updates locally without

16
00:01:21,892 --> 00:01:25,773
another model request.

17
00:01:30,250 --> 00:01:35,481
Now we request ninety nine units. The source does not have enough. The operation is

18
00:01:35,481 --> 00:01:40,711
rejected, and both balances stay unchanged. The same checks apply to every generated app

19
00:01:40,711 --> 00:01:45,942
using this capability. The model describes behavior; the runtime enforces its rules.

20
00:01:48,250 --> 00:01:52,985
Next, we ask Meta to add a report page while keeping the fields and rules. The model

21
00:01:52,985 --> 00:01:57,721
sees the application definition, not the stored record values. We review the updated

22
00:01:57,721 --> 00:02:02,456
declaration before installation. Existing data stays in place.

23
00:02:10,250 --> 00:02:14,662
The new page presents the existing balances and a dispatch note. The tool has changed

24
00:02:14,662 --> 00:02:19,074
through a second prompt, while our records and transfer rule remain intact.

25
00:02:22,250 --> 00:02:26,674
We close the guest and start a fresh instance. The host checkpoint restores the evolved

26
00:02:26,674 --> 00:02:31,098
application and its exact records. This is saved recovery, not new generation. The

27
00:02:31,098 --> 00:02:35,523
application remains usable without another call to Meta.

28
00:02:38,250 --> 00:02:42,583
Our Rust kernel runs on emulated ARM hardware. The guest draws and executes

29
00:02:42,583 --> 00:02:46,917
applications. The host provides HTTPS and persistence. Apps share kernel privilege

30
00:02:46,917 --> 00:02:51,250
today; isolated app services are future work.

31
00:02:52,250 --> 00:02:56,987
Try the public saved demo without a key. Bring your own Meta key to create new tools.

